            <ArticleTitle>Ovarian torsion: opportunities to improve clinical management.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>683-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/01443615.2012.698331</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Ovarian torsion is an uncommon condition which may affect women of any age. In a significant number of women, it may be associated with a concomitant pathology such as a cyst. Presently, there is no single diagnostic tool with significant predictive value. Multimodal diagnostic tools hold promise of early accurate diagnosis; prospective studies are required to assess the validity of such tools. This would enable wider application of ovarian function preserving procedures. Further, an assessment of the psychological impact of oophorectomy should be studied in order to facilitate the optimal tailored care.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
